\description{
Turns the result of any mariposa analysis into a plain data frame that
can be saved with \code{\link[utils:write.table]{utils::write.csv()}}, combined with
\code{\link[dplyr:bind_rows]{dplyr::bind_rows()}} or used for plotting. \code{\link[tibble:as_tibble]{tibble::as_tibble()}} works
the same way (it calls \code{as.data.frame()}), \code{\link[=write_xlsx]{write_xlsx()}} writes the
table (plus the secondary tables SPSS shows next to it) to Excel, and
with the \pkg{broom} package loaded, \code{broom::tidy()} returns the table
with broom's column names.
}
\details{
\code{broom::tidy()} (available once \pkg{broom} is loaded) renames the
common columns to broom's conventions: \code{statistic}, \code{p.value},
\code{parameter} (or \code{num.df}/\code{den.df} for F tests), \code{estimate}, \code{conf.low},
\code{conf.high}, \code{std.error}, \code{adj.p.value} (post-hoc tests), \code{term}
(ANOVA tables), plus \code{method} and, where it applies, \code{alternative}.
\code{\link[=normality_test]{normality_test()}} results are tidied to one row per variable and test.
The regressions keep their own \code{tidy()}, \code{glance()} and \code{augment()}
methods.
}
